1
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:10,117
As the long exile waned, a deer bounded away with a Brāhmaṇa's sacred fire-sticks tangled in its antlers.

2
00:00:10,117 --> 00:00:17,132
The five Pāṇḍava brothers gave chase, until it melted into shadow and left them lost, and parched.

3
00:00:21,132 --> 00:00:26,657
Nakula, youngest and swift, found a still and shining lake.

4
00:00:26,657 --> 00:00:33,868
As he stooped to drink, a voice rose from the empty air: "This water is mine.

5
00:00:33,868 --> 00:00:36,678
Answer me first — then drink."

6
00:00:40,878 --> 00:00:44,801
Heedless with thirst, Nakula drank, and fell.

7
00:00:44,801 --> 00:00:51,252
Sahadeva went to look for him, and fell in turn; then Arjuna; then Bhīma —

8
00:00:51,252 --> 00:01:00,579
each ignoring the voice, each drinking, each lying still, until four brothers lay fallen like felled trees.

9
00:01:05,578 --> 00:01:13,431
At last Yudhiṣṭhira came, and found all four fallen. He grieved — yet saw no wound.

10
00:01:13,431 --> 00:01:21,851
When the bodiless voice rose again above the still water, he bowed his head: "Ask, Yakṣa.

11
00:01:21,851 --> 00:01:23,270
I will answer."

12
00:01:27,470 --> 00:01:34,643
"What is swifter than the wind?" — "The mind." "What is heavier than the earth?" —

13
00:01:34,643 --> 00:01:39,367
"A mother." Riddle upon riddle, he answered every one.

14
00:01:39,367 --> 00:01:44,004
Then came the deepest: "What is the greatest wonder?"

15
00:01:48,404 --> 00:01:54,766
And Yudhiṣṭhira answered: "Day upon day, the dead are borne to the House of Death —

16
00:01:54,766 --> 00:02:00,822
yet those who remain believe they will never die. That is the greatest wonder."

17
00:02:06,222 --> 00:02:13,387
The crane blazed, and shed its shape: this was Dharma, his own divine father, testing him all along.

18
00:02:13,387 --> 00:02:22,199
Pleased, he raised all four brothers to their feet, and granted the Pāṇḍavas a year in which no one would know their faces.
